+# XPerl stack compatibility
+
+On XPerl with `PERL_RC_STACK`, the XS compatibility wrapper duplicates
+arguments and moves return values when an XS function returns. A saved
+pointer into its temporary argument slots cannot be used by a deferred
+save-stack callback.
+
+`unwind`, `yield` and `leave` retain their return values in an owning AV
+before returning through the wrapper. The deferred callback pushes those
+values onto the reference-counted stack with `rpp_push_1`. The save stack
+owns the AV and releases it after the values have been restored. `yield`
+uses `leave_adjust_stacks` to move owned values and release overwritten
+slots before unwinding the target scope.
+
+`uplevel` gives its synthetic caller argument array ownership of its
+elements. It releases the old pad reference when replacing the array,
+and clears the synthetic array when restoring the caller. This cleanup
+is needed even when the callback has no argument frame. A separate
+reference protects the synthetic array until restoration.
+
+These XS changes are conditional on both `XPERL_VERSION` and
+`PERL_RC_STACK`. Ordinary Perl retains the existing implementation.
+The code passes interpreter context explicitly and uses the existing
+per-interpreter storage for threaded builds. It does not convert NVs or
+assume an NV representation.
+
+The exception-location test compares the error with the target frame's
+`caller` location. This accommodates compilers that associate a multiline
+call with its first line or its last line.
+
+## Validation
+
+Use the interpreter being targeted to configure and run the tests:
+
+```sh
+perl Makefile.PL
+make
+PERL_DESTRUCT_LEVEL=2 make test
+```
+
+The repair passed all 55 test files with installed XPerl 5.45.5 on a
+non-threaded reference-counted stack build (89,441 tests), and on a
+threaded reference-counted stack build (89,790 tests). Supplementary
+ordinary Perl runs passed on 5.42.2 with threads and DEBUGGING, and on
+5.44.0 with threads and quadmath (89,790 tests each).
+
+`t/56-return-lifetime.t` checks object survival and release through the
+three deferred returns and through caller argument cleanup. Against the
+original release on the non-threaded XPerl build, it fails after its
+first assertion with an attempt to copy a freed scalar.
+
+Long-double builds and XPerl DEBUGGING builds were not tested. The
+ordinary Perl checks do not claim support for its experimental
+reference-counted stack configuration.
